# Story: someone who would rather use the mouse moves the view with double-clicks. A docked window's title
# bar frames that window, and the same double-click again goes back. Anywhere else a double-click
# belongs to the app under it. A tile opens on the first click, so the second click of a double-click on
# one goes nowhere instead of landing in the window just framed.
# Reference: T40 T51. The display is 1440x900 with a 25-point menu bar and 12-point gaps.
# Off as Arbor comes, and under the switch for everything that needs an event tap.
set pointerGestures true
expect ok
set titleBarDoubleClick true
expect ok
open Safari
open Notes
open Mail 300x200
dock 101 left
dock 102 right
# 101 is at 12,37 (464x851), the Desktop at 488,37, 102 at 964,37; 103 floats at 488,200.
dblclick 200 50
expect ok
expect camera 101
expect fills 101
expect hidden 102
expect hidden 103
# Its title bar is at the top of the display now.
dblclick 900 50
expect ok
expect camera root
expect visible 102
expect visible 103
# A window's content, a floating window's title bar and content, the gap between two spaces.
dblclick 200 400
expect fail
dblclick 600 210
expect fail
dblclick 600 300
expect fail
dblclick 482 400
expect fail
# The bare Desktop (T39) has a switch of its own, `desktopDoubleClick`, which is off.
dblclick 720 800
expect fail
expect camera root
# Xcode wants more room than its neighbours can spare and becomes a tile: n4 (n1 = 101, n2 = everything, n3 = 102, n4 = 104, n5 = column).
open Xcode 700x500 min 1200x600
dock 104 beside 102 bottom
expect tile 104
tile n4 2
expect ok
expect camera 104
expect fills 104
